{"cuisine/canadian-chinese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/canadian-chinese-cuisine","n":"Canadian Chinese cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Canadian cuisine","cuisine/canadian-cuisine"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in Canada","intro":[["Canadian Chinese cuisine is a cuisine derived from Chinese cuisine that was developed by Chinese Canadians. It was the first form of commercially available Chinese food in Canada. This cooking style was invented by early Cantonese immigrants who adapted traditional Chinese recipes to Western tastes and the available ingredients, and developed in a similar process to American Chinese cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"canadian-chinese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canadian_Chinese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Canadian cuisine","cuisine/canadian-cuisine"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Canadian cuisine","cuisine/canadian-cuisine"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/c/c1/Orange_Ginger_Beef_%282410442919%29.jpg/500px-Orange_Ginger_Beef_%282410442919%29.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Mack Male from Edmonton, AB, Canada","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orange_Ginger_Beef_(2410442919).jpg"}},"cuisine/cretan-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cretan-cuisine","n":"Cretan cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Greek cuisine","cuisine/greek-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Greece","intro":[["Cretan cuisine is the traditional cuisine of the Greek island of Crete."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cretan-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cretan_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Stuffed leaves","dish/stuffed-leaves"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Explore Crete: Cretan cooking recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"explorecrete.com","note":"Traditional Cretan recipes drawn from the work of Cretan food writer Nikos Psilakis.","url":"https://www.explorecrete.com/cuisine/psilakis-recipes.html","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Aegean: Recipes from the Mountains to the Sea","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Marianna Leivaditaki","note":"Cretan-born chef's recipes from her island upbringing, published by Interlink.","url":"https://www.simonandschuster.ca/books/Aegean/Marianna-Leivaditaki/9781623718237","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781623718237"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Grape leaves","ingredient/grape-leaves"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Greek cuisine","cuisine/greek-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/9/9f/Japrak_1.jpg/500px-Japrak_1.jpg","w":500,"h":329,"by":"Tizilohja25","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japrak_1.jpg","of":"Stuffed leaves"}},"ingredient/ragu":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/ragu","n":"Ragù","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 1 cuisine","intro":[["In Italian cuisine, ragù is a meat sauce commonly served with pasta. An Italian gastronomic society, Accademia Italiana della Cucina, has documented several ragù recipes. The recipes' common characteristics are the presence of meat and the fact that all are sauces for pasta. The most typical is ragù alla bolognese (Bolognese sauce). Other types include ragù alla napoletana (Neapolitan ragù), ragù di salsiccia, ragù alla barese (ragù from Bari, sometimes made with horse meat), and ragù d'anatra (a traditionally tomatoless duck ragù, from Veneto)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-ragu"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rag%C3%B9"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Arancini","dish/arancini"],["Lasagna","dish/lasagna"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Breadcrumbs","ingredient/breadcrumbs"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Béchamel sauce","ingredient/bechamel-sauce"],["Parmesan","ingredient/parmesan"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/b/b4/Ragu.jpg/500px-Ragu.jpg","w":500,"h":480,"by":"Erikatrioschi","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ragu.jpg"}},"ingredient/lime":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/lime","n":"Lime","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 12 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["The Key lime, also known as West Indian lime, Mexican lime, or Egyptian lime (Citrus × aurantiifolia) is a type of lime. While it is treated as a species in botanical classification, it originated as a natural hybrid between Citrus micrantha (a wild papeda) and Citrus medica (citron)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-lime"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Key_lime"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Ayam Taliwang","dish/ayam-taliwang"],["Betutu","dish/betutu"],["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Dengke mas naniura","dish/dengke-mas-naniura"],["Lawar (food)","dish/lawar-food"],["Manuk napinadar","dish/manuk-napinadar"],["Mie celor","dish/mie-celor"],["Saksang","dish/saksang"],["Satay","dish/satay"],["Sate klatak","dish/sate-klatak"],["Sate taichan","dish/sate-taichan"]]],["Mexican cuisine","cuisine/mexican-cuisine",[["Sopa de lima","dish/sopa-de-lima"]]],["Singaporean Chinese cuisine","cuisine/singaporean-cuisine-chinese-inspired",[["Satay","dish/satay"]]],["Singaporean cuisine","cuisine/singaporean-cuisine",[["Satay","dish/satay"]]]]},{"h":"Where it's grown","grown":{"what":"Lemons and limes","years":"2020 to 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QCL","world":22113897,"top":[["India","cuisine/indian-cuisine",3726745],["Mexico","cuisine/mexican-cuisine",3105561],["China","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",2302252],["Argentina","cuisine/argentine-cuisine",1955298],["Brazil","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine",1632185],["Turkey","cuisine/turkish-cuisine",1623449],["Spain","cuisine/spanish-cuisine",1009890],["United States of America","cuisine/american-cuisine",932224],["South Africa","cuisine/south-african-cuisine",727515],["Iran","cuisine/iranian-cuisine",505948],["Colombia","cuisine/colombian-cuisine",484959],["Italy","cuisine/italian-cuisine",464226],["Sudan","cuisine/sudanese-cuisine",377263],["Egypt","cuisine/egyptian-cuisine",376668],["Peru","cuisine/peruvian-cuisine",348109]]}},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Andaliman","ingredient/andaliman"],["Shallot","ingredient/shallot"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Offal","ingredient/offal"],["Peanut","ingredient/peanut"],["Scallion","ingredient/scallion"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/2/24/Citrus_%C3%97_aurantiifolia_%28Christm.%29_Swingle_%2851906868474%29.jpg/500px-Citrus_%C3%97_aurantiifolia_%28Christm.%29_Swingle_%2851906868474%29.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Dinesh Valke from Thane, India","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Citrus_%C3%97_aurantiifolia_(Christm.)_Swingle_(51906868474).jpg"}},"dish/baba-ghanoush":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/baba-ghanoush","n":"Baba ghanoush","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 5 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Baba ghanoush, also spelled baba ganoush or baba ghanouj, is a Levantine appetizer consisting of finely chopped roasted eggplant (aubergine), olive oil,  lemon juice, various seasonings, and tahini. The eggplant is traditionally roasted, baked or broiled over an open flame before peeling so that the pulp is soft and has a smoky taste. It is a typical meze (starter) of the regional cuisine, often served as a side to a main meal and as a dip for pita bread."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-baba-ghanoush"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baba_ghanoush"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Arab cuisine","cuisine/arab-cuisine"],["Armenian cuisine","cuisine/armenian-cuisine"],["Greek cuisine","cuisine/greek-cuisine"],["Israeli cuisine","cuisine/israeli-cuisine"],["Turkish cuisine","cuisine/turkish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Eggplant","ingredient/eggplant"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Tahini","ingredient/tahini"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"The Armenian Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"thearmeniankitchen.com","note":"Armenian-American blog of family recipes and reviews of Armenian cookbooks.","url":"https://www.thearmeniankitchen.com/classic-armenian-recipes-another/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Dimitra's Dishes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dimitrasdishes.com","note":"Dimitra Alfieris's video channel and blog focused on classic Greek home cooking.","url":"https://dimitrasdishes.com","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"Ozlem's Turkish Table","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"ozlemsturkishtable.com","note":"Turkish-born cook and teacher Ozlem Warren's blog of home-style Turkish recipes.","url":"https://ozlemsturkishtable.com/tag/turkish-recipes/page/9","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Arab cuisine","links":[["Kibbeh safarjaliyeh","dish/kibbeh-safarjaliyeh"],["Ma'amoul","dish/ma-amoul"],["Meghli","dish/meghli"],["Shakshouka","dish/shakshouka"],["Shawarma","dish/shawarma"],["Torshi","dish/torshi"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/1/1e/Noura_Restaurant_%283385996373%29.jpg/500px-Noura_Restaurant_%283385996373%29.jpg","w":500,"h":379,"by":"Serge Melki from Indianapolis, USA","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Noura_Restaurant_(3385996373).jpg"}},"ingredient/pickled-cucumber":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/pickled-cucumber","n":"Pickled cucumber","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 6 dishes across 7 cuisines","intro":[["A pickled cucumber – commonly known as a pickle in the United States and Canada and a gherkin ( GUR-kin) in Britain, Ireland, South Africa, Australia and New Zealand – is a usually small or miniature cucumber that has been pickled in a brine, vinegar, or other solution and left to ferment. The fermentation process is executed either by immersing the cucumbers in an acidic solution or through souring by lacto-fermentation. Pickled cucumbers are often part of mixed pickles."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-pickled-cucumber"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pickled_cucumber"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":7,"groups":[["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Rassolnik","dish/rassolnik"],["Solyanka","dish/solyanka"],["Vinegret","dish/vinegret"]]],["Soviet cuisine","cuisine/soviet-cuisine",[["Solyanka","dish/solyanka"],["Vinegret","dish/vinegret"]]],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",[["Solyanka","dish/solyanka"],["Vinegret","dish/vinegret"]]],["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"]]],["Belarusian cuisine","cuisine/belarusian-cuisine",[["Solyanka","dish/solyanka"]]],["Brazilian cuisine","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine",[["Bauru (sandwich)","dish/bauru-sandwich"]]],["Minnesotan cuisine","cuisine/minnesotan-cuisine",[["Minnesota sushi","dish/minnesota-sushi"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Bun","ingredient/bun"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Mozzarella","ingredient/mozzarella"],["Pão francês","ingredient/pao-frances"],["Roast beef","ingredient/roast-beef"],["Celery salt","ingredient/celery-salt"],["Chicago-style relish","ingredient/chicago-style-relish"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Hot dog","ingredient/hot-dog"],["Kosher dill pickle","ingredient/kosher-dill-pickle"],["Mustard","ingredient/mustard"],["Mustard (condiment)","ingredient/mustard-condiment"],["Poppy seed","ingredient/poppy-seed"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/b/bb/Pickle.jpg/500px-Pickle.jpg","w":500,"h":172,"by":"Renee Comet (photographer)","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pickle.jpg"}},"dish/banh-bot-loc":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/banh-bot-loc","n":"Bánh bột lọc","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Vietnamese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Bánh bột lọc are small, clear-looking, chewy tapioca dumplings in Vietnamese cuisine that can be eaten as appetizers or small snacks. They are usually filled with shrimp and pork belly, often being topped with fried shallots and served with sweet chili fish sauce. They are said to have originated from Huế, as the city was once the imperial capital of the Nguyễn dynasty and known for having simple, yet sophisticated dishes."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-banh-bot-loc"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B%C3%A1nh_b%E1%BB%99t_l%E1%BB%8Dc"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Vietnamese cuisine","cuisine/vietnamese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Pork belly","ingredient/pork-belly"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"],["Tapioca","ingredient/tapioca"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Viet World Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"vietworldkitchen.com","note":"Andrea Nguyen's blog on Vietnamese cooking, ingredients and technique.","url":"https://vietworldkitchen.com/blog/category/recipes-vietnamese","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Hungry Huy","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"hungryhuy.com","note":"Vietnamese-American Huy Vu's recipes for home-style Vietnamese dishes.","url":"https://www.hungryhuy.com/vietnamese/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Vietnamese cuisine","links":[["Bánh bao","dish/banh-bao"],["Bánh giầy","dish/banh-giay"],["Bánh tráng","dish/banh-trang"],["Chả rươi","dish/cha-ruoi"],["Chả trứng","dish/cha-trung"],["Chạo tôm","dish/chao-tom"],["Coconut candy","dish/coconut-candy"],["Cốm","dish/com"],["Khô cá lóc","dish/kho-ca-loc"],["Mắm nêm","dish/mam-nem"],["Nước chấm","dish/nuoc-cham"],["Ốc vú nàng","dish/oc-vu-nang"],["Shaking beef","dish/shaking-beef"],["Tương","dish/tuong"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/6/68/B%C3%A1nh_b%C3%A8o%2C_b%E1%BB%99t_l%E1%BB%8Dc%2C_nem_chua.jpg/500px-B%C3%A1nh_b%C3%A8o%2C_b%E1%BB%99t_l%E1%BB%8Dc%2C_nem_chua.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Thang Nguyen from Nottingham, United Kingdom","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:B%C3%A1nh_b%C3%A8o%2C_b%E1%BB%99t_l%E1%BB%8Dc%2C_nem_chua.jpg"}},"dish/clam-cake":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/clam-cake","n":"Clam cake","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Cuisine of New England","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Clam cakes (also known as clam fritters) are a part of New England cuisine, most commonly found in Rhode Island although they can also be found in Connecticut, Maine, and Massachusetts. They are balls of battered clams which have been deep-fried. On the Maine coast, clam cakes are formed into large, flat patties and fried."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-clam-cake"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clam_cake"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Cuisine of New England","cuisine/cuisine-of-new-england"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Clam","ingredient/clam"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Yankee Magazine: Chowder Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"newengland.com","note":"Recipes from Yankee, the long-running New England regional magazine.","url":"https://newengland.com/food/soups-stews-chowders/favorite-chowder-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"magazine"}]},{"h":"More from Cuisine of New England","links":[["Beach pizza","dish/beach-pizza"],["Boston baked beans","dish/boston-baked-beans"],["Parker House roll","dish/parker-house-roll"],["Thanksgiving sandwich","dish/thanksgiving-sandwich"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/0/09/Clam_cakes.jpg/500px-Clam_cakes.jpg","w":500,"h":229,"by":"Waz8","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clam_cakes.jpg"}},"ingredient/parsnip":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/parsnip","n":"Parsnip","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["The parsnip (Pastinaca sativa) is a root vegetable closely related to carrot and parsley, all belonging to the flowering plant family Apiaceae. It is a biennial plant usually grown as an annual. Its long taproot has light cream colored skin and flesh, and, left in the ground to mature, becomes sweeter in flavor after winter frosts. In its first growing season, the plant has a rosette of pinnate, mid-green leaves. If unharvested, it produces a flowering stem topped by an umbel of small yellow flowers in its second growing season, later producing pale brown, flat, winged seeds. By this time, the stem has become woody, and the taproot inedible."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-parsnip"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parsnip"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine",[["Eintopf","dish/eintopf"]]],["Thuringian cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine-thuringia",[["Eintopf","dish/eintopf"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bean","ingredient/bean"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Beer","ingredient/beer"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Wine","ingredient/wine"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/1/1f/Pastinaca_sativa_MHNT.BOT.2004.0.jpg/500px-Pastinaca_sativa_MHNT.BOT.2004.0.jpg","w":500,"h":412,"by":"Didier Descouens","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pastinaca_sativa_MHNT.BOT.2004.0.jpg"}},"dish/hojuela":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/hojuela","n":"Hojuela","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Spanish cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["An hojuela, also called an hojaldra, is a traditional Spanish and Latin American sweet fritter originating in Spain. In Latin American countries, especially Colombia, and in Spain, it is commonly made during Holy Week and the Christmas season. Ingredients vary by region, but usually consist of a flour-based batter fried in oil and dusted with sugar or topped with honey."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-hojuela"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hojuela"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Spain on a Fork","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"spainonafork.com","note":"Albert Bevia's video channel and site of traditional and everyday Spanish recipes.","url":"https://spainonafork.com/welcome2/","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"}]},{"h":"More from Spanish cuisine","links":[["Andrajos","dish/andrajos"],["Bolinhos de bacalhau","dish/bolinhos-de-bacalhau"],["Buñuelo","dish/bunuelo"],["Caldo galego","dish/caldo-galego"],["Chistorra","dish/chistorra"],["Churro","dish/churro"],["Cocido","dish/cocido"],["Cuajada","dish/cuajada"],["Embutido","dish/embutido"],["Gazpacho","dish/gazpacho"],["Jamón","dish/jamon"],["Migas","dish/migas"],["Mojo (sauce)","dish/mojo-sauce"],["Patatas a lo pobre","dish/patatas-a-lo-pobre"],["Patatas bravas","dish/patatas-bravas"],["Picatostes","dish/picatostes"],["Pincho","dish/pincho"],["Pottage","dish/pottage"],["Salchichón","dish/salchichon"],["Salmorejo","dish/salmorejo"],["Sobrassada","dish/sobrassada"],["Soldaditos de Pavía","dish/soldaditos-de-pavia"],["Sopa de gato","dish/sopa-de-gato"],["Spanish omelette","dish/spanish-omelette"],["Spanish restaurant","dish/spanish-restaurant"],["Stuffed peppers","dish/stuffed-peppers"],["Tapas","dish/tapas"],["Turrón","dish/turron"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/e/ea/Hojuelas.JPG/500px-Hojuelas.JPG","w":500,"h":281,"by":"User:Malejotm","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hojuelas.JPG"}},"ingredient/shrimp-as-food":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/shrimp-as-food","n":"Shrimp as food","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Shrimps and prawns are types of crustacean seafood that are consumed worldwide. Prawns and shrimps are crustacea and are very similar in appearance with the terms often used interchangeably in commercial farming and wild fisheries. A distinction was made in 1990s Indian aquaculture literature, which increasingly uses the term \"prawn\" only for the freshwater forms of palaemonids and \"shrimp\" for the marine penaeids that belong to different suborders of Decapoda. This has not been universally accepted."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-shrimp-as-food"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shrimp_as_food"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine",[["La tiang","dish/la-tiang"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/c/c3/Spicy_fried_King_Prawns.jpg/500px-Spicy_fried_King_Prawns.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"rovingI","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spicy_fried_King_Prawns.jpg"}},"dish/leningradsky-cake":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/leningradsky-cake","n":"Leningradsky cake","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Soviet cuisine","dot":"#a99fb5","intro":[["The Leningradsky cake is a Soviet pastry made of short-crust pastry, light chocolate buttercream, and cognac."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-leningradsky-cake"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leningradsky_cake"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Soviet cuisine","cuisine/soviet-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Buttercream","ingredient/buttercream"]]},{"h":"More from Soviet cuisine","links":[["Chicken tabaka","dish/chicken-tabaka"],["Dressed herring","dish/dressed-herring"],["Mimosa salad","dish/mimosa-salad"],["Olivier salad","dish/olivier-salad"],["Pelmeni","dish/pelmeni"],["Peremech","dish/peremech"],["Solyanka","dish/solyanka"],["Vinegret","dish/vinegret"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/f/f9/%D0%9B%